al_independence_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does conditional independence not imply ordinary independence in general?","options":["Ordinary independence is weaker than equality of probabilities","Conditional independence requires $A\\cap B=\\varnothing$","The event $C$ must be the full sample space","The factorization may hold only after restricting to $C$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c7d4faea3588d110f36a103f","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"replaced_after_academic_review"},{"id":"pt_batch4_conditional_independence_018","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Two events can be marginally dependent yet conditionally independent. Which mechanism explains this pattern?","options":["The events are impossible until the common cause is removed.","The shared factor makes all probabilities equal to one","A shared factor creates dependence; once known, no influence remains.","The events become complements after conditioning"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5f7529a0dc52c2eba5d14f87","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"pt_batch4_conditional_independence_019","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Given weather $C$, two location failures have probabilities $0.1$ and $0.2$ and are conditionally independent. What is the conditional probability that both fail?","options":["$0.30$","$0.10$","$0.80$","$0.02$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"bd895acad0ed4a1127d925bc","time_limit":40,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"pt_batch4_conditional_independence_020","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"If two answers are conditionally independent given